# cclock

Transparent, click-through clock overlay for X11.

`cclockctl` controls the running overlay without changing its service lifecycle:

```
cclockctl toggle
cclockctl show
cclockctl hide
cclockctl status
```

The control socket is `$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/cclock.sock`, or `/tmp/cclock-UID.sock`
when `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR` is unavailable.

When used with this `dwm` build, cclock renders the selected monitor's active
tag set before the digit sequence (for example, `3 0132131405` or
`1+3 0132131405`). While a Linux battery is discharging and exposes an energy
and draw-rate estimate through `/sys/class/power_supply`, the sequence starts
with its remaining time as `HHMM` only when it is below one hour; it is omitted
otherwise or when no estimate is available.

`CCLOCK_DATE_FORMAT` is rendered in `CCLOCK_DATE_*` colours. It defaults to
`"%d"`, preserving the original display. Set it to `"%m%d"` to highlight both
month and day; the remainder stays in `CCLOCK_TIME_FORMAT` and the base colour.

`CCLOCK_SECONDS_FORMAT` uses `CCLOCK_SECONDS_*` colours, and workspace tags use
`CCLOCK_WORKSPACE_*` colours. The render spans are workspace, remaining battery
time, date, hours/minutes, and seconds.

`CCLOCK_DYNAMIC_COLOURS` defaults to `1`. It selects a light or dark equivalent
of each configured span colour from the luminance of the root background beneath
it. This samples the root background, rather than the compositor's final scene,
so it is intended for wallpaper/root-colour contrast. Set it to `0` to use the
configured colours unchanged.

Colour profiles are selected in `src/config.h`. Both profiles now use a neutral
palette; `CCLOCK_THEME_GREYSCALE` is the default profile, with this visual
hierarchy:

1. Hours and minutes, then remaining battery time.
2. Date, then seconds.
3. Workspace.

Each adjacent span has a deliberate neutral step within that hierarchy. On a
dark backdrop, the main clock is brightest and workspace is dimmest. On a light
backdrop, the hierarchy reverses for legibility: hours/minutes are black,
battery is near-black, and the lower-priority spans become progressively
lighter greys. The adaptive equivalents preserve those steps instead of
compressing them, so parts remain clearly distinguishable in either mode.

This is a contrast principle, not merely a colour preference: saturated light
colours can disappear against a white surface, and a single fixed shade cannot
provide strong contrast on both pure black and pure white. Use greyscale with
dynamic equivalents to preserve the ordering on either root backdrop. Keep the
spans separate so the hierarchy remains readable; do not reintroduce bright
accent colours as a substitute for contrast. Rebuild and restart cclock after a
theme change.
